      "url": "https://www.last.fm/music/Suede/A+New+Morning",
      "wiki_content": "A New Morning is the fifth studio album by English alternative rock band Suede, released in September 2002. By the time the album was released, public interest in the band had waned, as shown by the poor charting of both the album and singles. Despite this, however, the album received moderate praise from critics and is generally liked by fans due to its warmer, and more simplistic sound (as opposed to Head Music). All the songs were produced by Stephen Street, except Positivity which was produced by John Leckie, and \"You Belong to Me\" which was produced by Dave Eringa. <a href=\"https://www.last.fm/music/Suede/A+New+Morning\">Read more on Last.fm</a>. User-contributed text is available under the Creative Commons By-SA License; additional terms may apply.",
      "wiki_summary": "A New Morning is the fifth studio album by English alternative rock band Suede, released in September 2002. By the time the album was released, public interest in the band had waned, as shown by the poor charting of both the album and singles. Despite this, however, the album received moderate praise from critics and is generally liked by fans due to its warmer, and more simplistic sound (as opposed to Head Music). All the songs were produced by Stephen Street, except Positivity which was produced by John Leckie, and \"You Belong to Me\" which was produced by Dave Eringa. <a href=\"https://www.last.fm/music/Suede/A+New+Morning\">Read more on Last.fm</a>."
    },
    "perplexity": {
      "description": "A New Morning is the fifth studio album by Suede, released in 2002 on Epic. It arrived after a long, expensive recording process and marked the band’s final studio album for more than a decade, with Stephen Street producing most of the record alongside John Leckie and Dave Eringa on select tracks.\n\nMusically, the album moves toward a warmer, more straightforward rock sound than its immediate predecessor, emphasizing melodic guitar pop, mid-tempo anthems, and reflective ballads. It was generally seen as a more optimistic record, shaped in part by Brett Anderson’s recovery from addiction, but it was also a commercial disappointment in the UK despite receiving moderate critical praise.",
